### TL;DR  
The shield layers do **not** look for a special “badge” property – they simply ask for an image whose **ID is the concatenation of the network name and the ref-length**:

```
icon-image: "{network}_{ref_length}"
```

So the exact sprite IDs that the three OSM-based shield layers expect are:

| Network (value of the `network` property) | Sprite IDs that will be requested |
|-------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| `us-interstate`                           | `us-interstate_1`  <br> `us-interstate_2`  <br> `us-interstate_3`  <br> `us-interstate_4`  <br> `us-interstate_5` |
| `us-highway`                              | `us-highway_1`  <br> `us-highway_2`  <br> `us-highway_3` |
| `us-state`                                | `us-state_1`  <br> `us-state_2`  <br> `us-state_3`  <br> `us-state_4`  <br> `us-state_5`  <br> `us-state_6` |
| (fallback for any other network)          | `road_1`  <br> `road_2`  <br> `road_3`  <br> `road_4`  <br> `road_5`  <br> `road_6` |

If any of those IDs are missing from the **sprite sheet** that your style points at, the layer can still draw the text (the route number) but the badge image is omitted – exactly the “bare floating numbers” you are seeing.

---

## How to make the badges appear **without touching the style layers**

You have three practical ways to supply those missing images. Choose the one that best fits your workflow.

### 1️⃣ Point the style at a sprite sheet that already contains the IDs

The easiest fix is to use a sprite that ships with the OSM-Bright / OpenMapTiles styles, because those sheets already contain the full set of shield images.

```json
{
  "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite"
}
```

*Why this works*  
The URL above resolves to three files:

```
…/sprite.json
…/sprite.png
…/sprite@2x.json
…/sprite@2x.png
```

The JSON index contains **all** of the IDs listed in the table (up to `us-interstate_5`, `us-highway_3`, `us-state_6`, `road_6`). When the style loads, MapLibre will automatically request the appropriate `@2x` variant on high-DPI screens.

**If you already have a custom sprite** (e.g., you merged your own icons into the provider’s sheet), just make sure the merged JSON includes the above IDs. You can verify with:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  console.log('Missing shield images:');
  ['us-interstate','us-highway','us-state','road'].forEach(net => {
    for (let i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
      const id = `${net}_${i}`;
      if (!map.hasImage(id)) console.log('  ❌', id);
    }
  });
});
```

If the console prints nothing, the sprite is complete.

### 2️⃣ Add a **second sprite sheet** to the style (MapLibre ≥ 3.0)

If you already have a custom sprite for your own icons and you don’t want to merge the shield images into it, you can keep your sheet **and** add the OSM-Bright shield sheet as a second entry.

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://example.com/my-icons/sprite" },
    { "id": "osm-bright", "url": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite" }
  ]
}
```

*How to reference the images*  

- Images from the `default` sheet keep their original un-prefixed names (`cafe`, `park`, …).  
- Images from the second sheet must be prefixed with the sheet’s `id`:

```json
"layout": {
  "icon-image": "osm-bright:us-interstate_2"
}
```

**BUT** you **don’t have to edit the existing shield layers**. MapLibre automatically resolves an un-prefixed name against the `default` sheet **only**. To make the existing layers see the new sheet **without editing them**, you can give the shield sheet the **id `default`** and move your own icons to a different id:

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "my-icons", "url": "https://example.com/my-icons/sprite" },
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite" }
  ]
}
```

Now the style’s original `icon-image` values (`us-interstate_2`, `road_3`, …) resolve against the `default` sheet (the OSM-Bright sheet) and your own icons are accessed as `my-icons:my-custom-icon`. No layer edits required.

You can also add the second sheet **programmatically** after the style loads:

```js
map.on('styledata', () => {
  // add the OSM-Bright sheet as the default (overwrites the existing default)
  map.addSprite('default', 'https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite');
});
```

> **Note:** `addSprite` can only be called **once** per id; calling it again with the same id will replace the previous sheet (which is fine if you want the OSM-Bright sheet to become the default).

### 3️⃣ Register the missing images at runtime with `addImage`

If you prefer not to touch the `sprite` property at all (e.g., you are serving a completely static style from a CDN you cannot modify), you can load the missing badge images yourself and add them to the map after the style has loaded.

```js
async function addShield(id, url) {
  // Load the PNG (or WebP) – MapLibre’s loadImage returns a Promise
  const { data } = await map.loadImage(url);
  if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, data);
}

// Example: add the three interstate badge sizes
map.on('load', async () => {
  await Promise.all([
    addShield('us-interstate_1', 'https://example.com/shields/us-interstate_1.png'),
    addShield('us-interstate_2', 'https://example.com/shields/us-interstate_2.png'),
    addShield('us-interstate_3', 'https://example.com/shields/us-interstate_3.png')
    // … add any others you need
  ]);
});
```

**Why this works**  
`addImage` registers the image under the exact ID that the layer’s `icon-image` expression will request. Since the layer already asks for `"us-interstate_2"` etc., once the image is present the badge appears automatically. No need to change the style JSON.

**Caveats**

- Each image is a separate HTTP request (PNG, WebP, or JPEG). If you have many badge sizes, the network overhead can be noticeable compared with a single sprite PNG+JSON.
- The image **must not be an SVG** unless you rasterize it yourself (create an `Image` element, wait for `onload`, then `addImage`). `loadImage` only accepts raster formats.
- If you need the badge to be stretchable (so a single image can serve all `ref_length`s), you must also supply the `stretchX`, `stretchY`, and `content` metadata when calling `addImage`. Example:

```js
map.addImage('road_stretch', roadImg, {
  stretchX: [[4, 20]],   // pixels that may stretch horizontally
  stretchY: [[4, 20]],
  content: [4, 4, 20, 20] // inner box where the text must fit
});
```

Then you would have to edit the layer to set `icon-text-fit: "both"` – which you said you want to avoid. So for a “no-layer-edit” fix, just add the pre-drawn badge PNGs.

---

## Quick checklist to get your shields back

1. **Confirm the sprite you are using actually contains the IDs**  
   ```js
   map.on('load', () => {
     console.log('us-interstate_2?', map.hasImage('us-interstate_2'));
   });
   ```
   If it logs `false`, the sprite is missing that badge.

2. **Swap to a known-good sprite** (the OSM-Bright one)  
   ```json
   "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite"
   ```
   Reload – the badges should appear.

3. **If you need to keep your own sprite**  
   - Add the OSM-Bright sheet as a second sprite with id `default` (or replace the existing default).  
   - Or, add the missing badge PNGs with `addImage` after `load`.

4. **Verify** – open the browser console and look for any `Image "<id>" could not be loaded` warnings. When none appear, the shields are fully resolved.

---

### Example: Minimal style that works out-of-the-box

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/tiles/osm/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
    }
  },
  "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite",
  "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "highway-shield-us-interstate",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation_name",
      "filter": ["==", ["get", "network"], "us-interstate"],
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}",
        "text-field": "{ref}",
        "text-font": ["Open Sans Bold"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "symbol-placement": "line"
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#fff"
      }
    }
    // … other layers …
  ]
}
```

With that `sprite` URL the shield badges (`us-interstate_1` … `us-interstate_5`) are present, so the numbers are rendered on top of the proper badge images.

