## TL;DR Summary

| Layer | Expected sprite IDs |
|-------|---------------------|
| US Interstate | `us-interstate_1` … `us-interstate_5` |
| US Highway   | `us-highway_1` … `us-highway_3` |
| US State     | `us-state_1` … `us-state_6` |
| Generic road | `road_1` … `road_6` |

**Fix options (no layer edits needed):**

1. Point `sprite` at a sheet that already contains those IDs (e.g., `https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite`).  
2. Add a second sprite sheet (id `default`) that contains the IDs, keeping your own icons on a different id.  
3. Load the missing badge PNGs yourself and register them with `map.addImage(id, data)` after the style loads.

Pick the approach that matches your deployment constraints, and the shields will render with their proper badge backgrounds again. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for getting your own POI icons to show up **without merging sprite sheets or rebuilding the basemap’s sheet**.  
It works with any recent version of MapLibre GL JS (≥ 3.0 – the version that introduced *multiple sprite sheets*). If you are on an older version you’ll need to upgrade first; the rest of the guide assumes you are on 3.x or later (the same code works on v6 as well).

---

## 1. Why the icons never appear

| What you have | What MapLibre expects |
|---------------|-----------------------|
| `style.sprite = "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap"` (a **single** string) | All `icon-image` values are looked up **only** in that one sprite sheet. |
| You added a second sheet on `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi` and set `icon-image: "cafe"` | MapLibre never asks that second URL, so the lookup fails silently (no error because the style never tried to load the image). |

The fix is to **tell the style about both sheets**. MapLibre now accepts an **array of sprite objects**:

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap" },
    { "id": "my-icons", "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  …
}
```

* `default` is a special id – its images are referenced **without a prefix** (exactly what the original style already does).  
* All other ids must be used as a **prefix** when you refer to an image from that sheet: `"my-icons:cafe"`.

---

## 2. Updating the style JSON

### 2.1. Edit the `sprite` property

If you control the style file (e.g. you host it yourself or you can edit the JSON that you pass to `new maplibregl.Map({ style })`), replace the single string with the array shown above.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm" },
    { "id": "my-icons", "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "cafes",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "source-layer": "poi",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-icons:cafe",   // <-- note the prefix
        "icon-size": 1.2
      }
    }
    // … other layers that still use the basemap’s icons, e.g. "hospital"
  ]
}
```

### 2.2. Keep the basemap’s icons unchanged

Because the basemap’s sheet is given the id **`default`**, all of its existing `icon-image` values (e.g. `"hospital"`, `"railway_station"`) keep working **without any change**.

---

## 3. Adding the extra sheet at runtime (optional)

If you cannot edit the style file (e.g. you receive the style from a third-party API), you can add the second sheet **after the map has loaded**:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://basemap.provider.com/style.json', // only the default sheet
  center: [-122.42, 37.77],
  zoom: 12
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the extra sprite sheet
  map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');

  // 2️⃣ (Optional) Verify it loaded
  map.once('error', e => console.error('Sprite load error:', e));
  map.once('data', e => {
    if (e.sourceId === 'my-icons' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
      console.log('✅ my-icons sprite sheet loaded');
    }
  });

  // 3️⃣ Add / update your POI layer (or any layer that uses the new icons)
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-pois',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://mydomain.com/geojson/pois.geojson'
    },
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'], // expects values like "my-icons:cafe"
      'icon-allow-overlap': true
    }
  });
});
```

**Key points**

* `addSprite(id, url)` works **exactly** like the array form in the style JSON – the same prefix rules apply.
* The `url` you pass **must not** contain a file extension. MapLibre will request `url.json`, `url.png`, and the `@2x` variants automatically.
* The call must happen **after** the style’s `load` (or `style.load`) event, otherwise the map will reject the request because the style isn’t ready to accept extra sprite sheets.

---

## 4. Referencing the icons in your layers

### 4.1. Prefix syntax

| Sprite sheet id | How to reference an image |
|-----------------|---------------------------|
| `default` (the basemap) | `"hospital"` |
| `my-icons` (your sheet) | `"my-icons:cafe"` |
| any other id you add | `"other-id:image-name"` |

If you use an expression to pull the name from a feature property, you must **prepend the prefix yourself**:

```js
// Assuming each feature has a property `icon` that contains just "cafe", "park", …
layout: {
  'icon-image': [
    'concat', 'my-icons:', ['get', 'icon']
  ]
}
```

### 4.2. Verify the image exists

After the map has loaded you can ask:

```js
if (map.hasImage('my-icons:cafe')) {
  console.log('cafe icon is ready');
} else {
  console.warn('cafe icon missing – check the sprite JSON');
}
```

`map.listImages()` will list **all** loaded IDs (including the `id:` prefix), which is handy for debugging.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to spot them

| Symptom | Likely cause | How to confirm |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| POI icons never appear, **no console warning** | Sprite sheet URL is wrong **or** missing the required “no-extension” form (`…/poi` instead of `…/poi.json`). | Open `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json` in a browser – you should get a JSON file with keys like `"cafe"`.<br>Check network tab for 404/403. |
| `map.hasImage('my-icons:cafe')` returns `false` **but** the JSON contains `"cafe"` | CORS block on the sprite PNG/JSON. | Look at the network tab – the PNG request should have `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your origin). |
| `map.hasImage('cafe')` is `true` but the icon is still invisible | The layer’s `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-optional` / `symbol-sort-key` are causing the icon to be culled. | Temporarily set `"icon-allow-overlap": true` and `"icon-ignore-placement": true` to force drawing. |
| You get `Invalid sprite URL "[object Object]"` | Your tooling still treats `sprite` as a string and stringifies the array. | Ensure any code that reads the style treats `sprite` as either a string **or** an array of objects. |
| You are on MapLibre 2.x | Multiple sprite sheets are not supported. | Upgrade to ≥ 3.0 (or use `addImage` for each POI icon). |

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – two sprite sheets</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://tiles.openfreemap.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
        tileSize: 256
      }
    },
    sprite: [
      { id: 'default', url: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm' },
      { id: 'my-icons', url: 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi' }
    ],
    layers: [
      { id: 'basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'basemap' },
      // Example POI layer that will use our custom icons
      {
        id: 'my-pois',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [
              {
                type: 'Feature',
                geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.77] },
                properties: { icon: 'cafe' }
              },
              {
                type: 'Feature',
                geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.43, 37.78] },
                properties: { icon: 'park' }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          // prepend the prefix at runtime
          'icon-image': ['concat', 'my-icons:', ['get', 'icon']],
          'icon-size': 1.2,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.42, 37.77],
  zoom: 13
});

map.on('load', () => {
  console.log('Map loaded – available images:', map.listImages());
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi` with the real base URL of your sprite sheet.*  
Open the page, open the browser console, and you should see something like:

```
Map loaded – available images: ["default:water", "default:road", "my-icons:cafe", "my-icons:park"]
```

Both custom icons will now render on the map.

---

## 7. If you cannot upgrade to ≥ 3.0

| Option | How it works |
|--------|--------------|
| **`addImage` for each POI icon** | Load the PNG/SVG at runtime (`map.loadImage` → `map.addImage`). Works fine for a handful of icons but incurs a separate request per image. |
| **Merge the two sheets** | Use `spritezero`, `spreet`, or `Martin` to combine the basemap PNG+JSON with your own PNG+JSON into a single sheet. This is the old-school approach; you said you’d rather avoid it. |
| **Proxy the basemap’s sprite** | Create a tiny server that forwards the basemap’s sprite requests *and* serves your own sheet under a different path, then point `style.sprite` at that proxy. This effectively gives you the array behaviour without changing the client code, but it adds a server component. |

Upgrading is usually the simplest and most future-proof solution.

---

## 8. Quick checklist to get it working

1. **Upgrade** to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0 (or v6, which also supports the array form).  
2. **Edit the style** (or call `map.addSprite`) so `sprite` is an **array** with two entries:  
   * `{ "id": "default", "url": "<basemap-sprite-base-url>" }`  
   * `{ "id": "my-icons", "url": "<your

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, rasterised into an `HTMLImageElement`, and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains ~5 000 point features, each with a `category` property that matches the icon id (`cafe`, `park`, …).  
* A single **symbol layer** draws the icons.  
* The default collision-detection (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) automatically hides icons that would otherwise pile up when you are zoomed out.  
* The code is written with modern ES-modules / async-await, so it works in any recent browser without a build step.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // a very light basemap – you can replace it with any style you like
      sources: {
        osm: {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.42, 37.77], // San Francisco (change to your area)
    zoom: 12,
    // make sure the map knows we will add images later
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an Image element
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  /**
   * Loads an SVG (or any raster image) from `url` and resolves with the
   * HTMLImageElement once it has finished decoding.
   *
   * @param {string} url  – absolute URL to the SVG file
   * @param {number} [width] – optional width in px (MapLibre respects the
   *                           element’s intrinsic size)
   * @param {number} [height] – optional height in px
   * @returns {Promise<HTMLImageElement>}
   */
  async function loadSvgImage(url, width = 24, height = 24) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const img = new Image(width, height);
      // Required for cross-origin SVGs; otherwise addImage throws.
      img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
      img.onload = () => resolve(img);
      img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load SVG ${url}`));
      img.src = url;
    });
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Load all icons (once the style has finished loading)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.once('style.load', async () => {
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3a️⃣  List of icon ids + URLs (adjust to your own host)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const icons = [
      // id   →   URL (relative to your domain, absolute works too)
      { id: 'cafe',      url: 'https://mydomain.com/icons/cafe.svg' },
      { id: 'park',      url: 'https://mydomain.com/icons/park.svg' },
      { id: 'library',   url: 'https://mydomain.com/icons/library.svg' },
      { id: 'museum',    url: 'https://mydomain.com/icons/museum.svg' },
      // … add the rest of your 40-ish icons here …
    ];

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3b️⃣  Load each SVG, then register it with the map.
    //      `hasImage` guards against duplicate ids (e.g. if the style
    //      already ships an image with the same name).
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    for (const {id, url} of icons) {
      if (map.hasImage(id)) {
        console.warn(`Image "${id}" already exists in the style – skipping`);
        continue;
      }
      try {
        const img = await loadSvgImage(url);
        // `addImage` options are optional; we keep the defaults.
        // `sdf: false` (default) → icon-color/halo have no effect.
        map.addImage(id, img);
        console.log(`✅ Added icon "${id}"`);
      } catch (e) {
        console.error(e);
      }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source with the 5 000 venues
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Replace the URL with the real location of your GeoJSON.
    const venuesUrl = 'https://mydomain.com/data/venues.geojson';

    map.addSource('venues', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: venuesUrl,
      // optional: clustering if you ever want it (not needed for icon-only)
      // cluster: true,
      // clusterRadius: 50
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  Symbol layer that draws the icons
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'venue-icons',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'venues',
      // The icon name comes from the feature property `category`.
      // Example feature: { "type":"Feature","properties":{"category":"cafe"},… }
      layout: {
        // Use an expression to read the property.
        // If a feature has a category that wasn't added above, the icon is omitted.
        'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

        // Keep the default collision handling (icon-allow-overlap: false)
        // – icons that would overlap are hidden.
        // If you ever want to force every icon to draw, set:
        // 'icon-allow-overlap': true,

        // Optional: size the icons (24 px is the SVG’s intrinsic size)
        // 'icon-size': 1,

        // Optional: give a little padding so icons don’t touch the map edge
        'icon-optional': false
      },
      paint: {
        // No SDF → `icon-color` does nothing. If you need a single-color
        // silhouette you would have to generate an SDF sprite instead.
      }
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  (Optional) Fit the map to the data once it’s loaded
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.once('data', (e) => {
      if (e.sourceId === 'venues' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
        const bounds = new maplibregl.LngLatBounds();
        const source = map.getSource('venues');
        // `source._data` is the raw GeoJSON (available after load)
        source._data.features.forEach(f => bounds.extend(f.geometry.coordinates));
        map.fitBounds(bounds, { padding: 40, maxZoom: 15 });
      }
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | A minimal raster basemap is used; you can swap in any style (vector, raster, etc.). | Guarantees the map is ready before we start loading images. |
| **2️⃣ `loadSvgImage()`** | Creates an `Image` element, sets `crossOrigin='anonymous'`, waits for `onload`. | `map.addImage()` only accepts raster `Image` objects; SVGs must be rasterised first. |
| **3️⃣ Load & register icons** | Loops over the 40 + icon definitions, skips duplicates, registers each with `map.addImage(id, img)`. | After registration the id can be referenced in a style (`icon-image`). |
| **4️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source** | Points are loaded from a remote file (or you could embed the GeoJSON directly). | The source supplies the 5 000 venue features. |
| **5️⃣ Symbol layer** | `icon-image` is an expression `['get','category']`. The map looks up the `category` property of each feature and uses that string as the image id. | One layer draws *all* venues; no need for 5 000 separate layers. |
| **Collision handling** | The default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) hides icons that would overlap, preventing “piling up” when zoomed out. | Meets the requirement that icons not clutter the view at low zoom. |
| **6️⃣ Fit bounds (optional)** | After the source finishes loading, the map zooms to show all venues. | Handy for demos; can be removed in production. |

### Things to keep in mind

* **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). Otherwise `addImage` will throw a security error.
* **Image size** – The SVG’s intrinsic width/height become the icon’s pixel size. If you need larger icons, either create larger SVGs or set `'icon-size': <scale>` in the layer layout.
* **SDF icons** – If you ever want the icons to be recolourable with `icon-color`/`icon-halo-*`, generate an SDF sprite (or convert the SVG to an SDF PNG) and register it with `{sdf: true}`. For plain colour SVGs the default (`sdf: false`) is fine.
* **Performance** – Registering 40 + images is cheap (one PNG request per icon). The heavy work is the 5 000 point features, which MapLibre handles efficiently as a single source/layer.

That’s it! Drop the HTML into a file, adjust the icon URLs and the GeoJSON URL, and you’ll have a fully functional MapLibre map where each venue shows its category icon, with automatic collision avoidance when you zoom out. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a draggable pin (a `Marker`) at a known longitude/latitude**  
2. **Attach a `Popup` that opens when the user clicks the pin**  
3. **Update the stored coordinate when the pin is dragged**  

It works with any recent version of **MapLibre GL JS** (≥ 3.0, the version that supports the array-form `sprite` but the code does not depend on that).  

---

## 1️⃣ HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin with popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <!-- MapLibre CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the version (`3.2.0`) with whatever you are using; the CDN always serves the latest stable release.*

---

## 2️⃣ `app.js` – the JavaScript

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
  center: [-122.42, 37.77],             // initial view (lon, lat)
  zoom: 12
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Wait until the style is fully loaded before adding UI
// ------------------------------------------------------------
map.on('load', () => {
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Define the starting coordinate for the pin
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  const startLngLat = [-122.42, 37.77]; // San Francisco downtown

  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Create a Popup that will be shown on click
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
    offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
    closeButton: true,
    closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
  }).setHTML(`
    <h3>My draggable pin</h3>
    <p>Coordinates: <span id="coord">${startLngLat[0].toFixed(5)}, ${startLngLat[1].toFixed(5)}</span></p>
    <p>Drag the pin to move it.</p>
  `);

  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Create the draggable Marker
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
    draggable: true,          // <-- makes it draggable
    // optional: give it a custom element or an image
    // element: document.createElement('div')
  })
    .setLngLat(startLngLat)   // place it at the start coordinate
    .setPopup(popup)          // bind the popup (opens on click automatically)
    .addTo(map);              // finally add it to the map

  // --------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣ Keep the popup text in sync when the marker is dragged
  // --------------------------------------------------------
  function onDragEnd() {
    const lngLat = marker.getLngLat(); // {lng:…, lat:…}
    // Update the coordinate display inside the popup
    const coordSpan = document.getElementById('coord');
    if (coordSpan) {
      coordSpan.textContent = `${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`;
    }

    // If you need the new position elsewhere (e.g. send to a server)
    console.log('Marker dropped at', lngLat);
  }

  // Register the drag-end listener
  marker.on('dragend', onDragEnd);
});
```

### What each part does

| Step | Code | Why it matters |
|------|------|----------------|
| **1** | `new maplibregl.Map({...})` | Creates the map canvas and loads a style. |
| **2** | `map.on('load', …)` | Guarantees the style (including sprites) is ready before we add UI. |
| **3** | `const startLngLat = …` | The initial longitude/latitude for the pin. |
| **4** | `new maplibregl.Popup({...}).setHTML(...)` | A popup that will appear when the marker is clicked. The HTML contains a `<span id="coord">` that we later update. |
| **5** | `new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})` | The **draggable pin**. `setLngLat` places it, `setPopup` binds the popup, `addTo(map)` puts it on the map. |
| **6** | `marker.on('dragend', onDragEnd)` | Fires after the user finishes dragging. We read the new coordinates with `marker.getLngLat()` and update the popup (or send the data elsewhere). |

---

## 3️⃣ Customising the pin (optional)

If you want a different icon than the default gray pin, you have two easy options:

### A. Use a custom HTML element

```js
const el = document.createElement('div');
el.style.backgroundImage = 'url(https://example.com/my-pin.svg)';
el.style.width = '32px';
el.style.height = '32px';
el.style.backgroundSize = '100%';

const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
  .setLngLat(startLngLat)
  .setPopup(popup)
  .addTo(map);
```

### B. Use a sprite image (`addImage`)

If you already have a sprite sheet (or want to load a single PNG/SVG at runtime) you can register it with `map.addImage()` and then create a marker that references that image:

```js
// after map.load
await map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
  .then(({ data }) => {
    if (!map.hasImage('my-pin')) map.addImage('my-pin', data);
  });

const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  draggable: true,
  // `element` is still required for a Marker, but we can set its background
  element: document.createElement('div')
});
marker.getElement().style.backgroundImage = 'url(data:image/png;base64,…)';
marker.getElement().style.width = '32px';
marker.getElement().style.height = '32px';
```

> **Tip:** `Marker` does **not** read `icon-image` from a sprite; you must supply an HTML element (or use the default pin). If you need many points that share the same icon, use a **symbol layer** with a sprite instead of a `Marker`.

---

## 4️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Pin never appears | `map` not loaded yet, or `setLngLat` called before `load` | Wrap marker creation in `map.on('load', …)` (as shown). |
| Popup does not open on click | `setPopup` not called, or `closeOnClick:true` and you click elsewhere immediately | Ensure `marker.setPopup(popup)` is executed; `closeOnClick:false` keeps it open after a click on the map. |
| Dragging works but the coordinate shown never changes | `onDragEnd` not attached, or you are updating the wrong DOM element | Verify `marker.on('dragend', onDragEnd)` and that the `<span id="coord">` exists. |
| Custom pin image is missing or broken | Wrong URL, CORS, or using a raw-GitHub URL (rate-limited) | Host the image on a proper static CDN (e.g., GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare) and make sure it returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`. |
| Marker “jumps” after a drag | The map’s `pitch` or `bearing` changes while dragging (rare) | Use `marker.setOffset([0,0])` or disable map rotation while dragging (`dragRotate: false` in map options). |

---

## 5️⃣ Full-page example (copy-paste)

If you want a single file to test locally, put everything in `index.html`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin with popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    html,body,#map{margin:0;padding:0;height:100%;}
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    const start = [-122.42, 37.77];
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset:25, closeButton:true, closeOnClick:false })
      .setHTML(`
        <h3>Draggable pin</h3>
        <p>Coordinates: <span id="coord">${start[0].toFixed(5)}, ${start[1].toFixed(5)}</span></p>
      `);

    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable:true })
      .setLngLat(start)
      .setPopup(popup)
      .addTo(map);

    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      document.getElementById('coord').textContent =
        `${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`;
      console.log('New position:', lngLat);
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open that file in a browser (or serve it via a local web server) and you’ll see a draggable pin. Clicking it opens the popup; dragging updates the displayed coordinates.

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR checklist

- **Create the map** → `new maplibregl.Map(...)`.
- **Wait for `load`** before adding UI.
- **Make a `Popup`** (`new maplibregl.Popup()`) with the HTML you want.
- **Create a `Marker`** with `{draggable:true}`.
- **Bind the popup** → `marker.setPopup(popup)`.
- **Add the marker** → `marker.addTo(map)`.
- **Listen for `dragend`** → `marker.on('dragend', …)` to read the new `lngLat`.
- **(Optional) Use a custom element** if you don’t like the default pin.

That’s all you need to place a draggable pin on a MapLibre GL JS map and show a popup when the user clicks it. Happy mapping!
